TITLE:: PlotLib
summary:: Palette, fonts and the pure helpers shared by every plot
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/PltView

DESCRIPTION::
Namespace for the parts of PlotLib that have no GUI in them, so all of it can be
tested headless. See link::Overviews/PlotLib:: for the library as a whole.

SECTION:: Themes

Three themes are built in:

DEFINITIONLIST::
## code::\darkBlue:: || the default: the Accrete/Ora ground with a blue accent.
## code::\dark:: || neutral graphite, where the data carries the colour and the
frame does not.
## code::\light:: || for print and for slides on a white ground.
::

code::
PlotLib.theme_(\light);      // every open plot changes with it
PlotLib.theme_(\darkBlue);
::

Views read the palette while drawing rather than at creation, and open windows are
kept in code::PlotLib.views::, so switching theme redraws them all in place. A
colour set explicitly on an instance (code::plot.dotColor_(Color.red)::) survives a
theme change; a colour left alone follows the theme.

note::
A palette is not invertible, and two things have to change with the ground rather
than being flipped: an alpha blend darkens on a dark ground and washes out on a
light one, and a 1px anti-aliased line that reads on dark nearly disappears on
white. strong::alphaFor:: and strong::lineWidth:: handle both, and every view puts
its alphas and hairlines through them.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: theme
The name of the current theme, and the setter that switches it. Switching
rethemes every open view; an unknown name warns and changes nothing.
argument:: name
code::\darkBlue::, code::\dark::, code::\light::, or one added with
strong::addTheme::.

METHOD:: addTheme
Add or replace a theme. A palette needs code::ground:: (code::\dark:: or
code::\light::) and the nine colour keys.
code::
(
PlotLib.addTheme(\amber, (
	ground: \dark,
	bg:    Color.new255(20, 16, 12),
	ink:   Color.new255(222, 160, 74),
	mint:  Color.new255(150, 200, 150),
	gold:  Color.new255(240, 210, 120),
	rose:  Color.new255(210, 120, 120),
	trace: Color.new255(240, 232, 220),
	muted: Color.new255(160, 145, 125),
	edge:  Color.new255(90, 78, 62),
	grid:  Color(1, 1, 1, 0.06)
));
PlotLib.theme_(\amber);
)
::

METHOD:: themes
Every theme, as an Event of palettes.

METHOD:: views
Open views, so that a theme change can reach them. A view adds itself when it opens
and removes itself when it closes.

METHOD:: color
The palette colour for a key, falling back to code::\ink:: for an unknown one.
Keys: code::bg::, code::ink::, code::mint::, code::gold::, code::rose::,
code::trace::, code::muted::, code::edge::, code::grid::.
argument:: key
A Symbol.

METHOD:: setColor
Replace one entry of the current theme. This edits the theme itself, so it holds
across a switch away and back.
code::
PlotLib.setColor(\ink, Color(0.9, 0.6, 0.3));
::

METHOD:: isLight
Whether the current theme sits on a light ground.

METHOD:: alphaFor
An alpha value adjusted for the ground. Transparency encodes density in the scatter
plots, and the same number does not mean the same weight on white as on black.

METHOD:: lineWidth
A stroke width adjusted for the ground: a hairline needs about double the weight to
read on white.

METHOD:: palette
The whole palette of the current theme, as an Event.

METHOD:: font
A Font in the resolved interface family.
argument:: size
argument:: bold

METHOD:: fontName
The family actually in use, resolved on first call from a preference list.
code::Font.availableFonts:: lists families rather than faces, so a name that does
not exist falls back silently; resolving from a list avoids naming one.

METHOD:: map
Rescale code::value:: from one range to another, clipped to the target. A
degenerate input range gives code::outLo:: instead of dividing by zero.
argument:: value
argument:: inLo
argument:: inHi
argument:: outLo
argument:: outHi

METHOD:: histogram
Count values into equal bins across code::[lo, hi]::. Values at code::hi:: land in
the last bin; non-numbers and NaN are skipped.
returns:: An Array of counts, code::bins:: long.

METHOD:: extent
strong::min:: and strong::max:: of a collection in one pass, ignoring NaN. Returns
code::nil:: for an empty or all-NaN input, and widens a constant input so no
mapping ever divides by zero.
returns:: code::[lo, hi]:: or nil.

METHOD:: ticks
Tick values inside code::[lo, hi]::, spaced 1, 2, 2.5 or 5 times a power of ten.
argument:: lo
argument:: hi
argument:: maxCount
About how many ticks are wanted; the step is chosen to come near it.

METHOD:: fmt
A compact fixed-point string for an axis label, with the precision taken from the
span it belongs to. Whole quanta print as integers, so a frequency axis reads
code::5000:: and not code::5000.0::.

METHOD:: ampDb
Amplitude to dB with a floor, so silence maps to the bottom of a meter rather than
to negative infinity.

METHOD:: classes
Every class in the quark.

METHOD:: version

EXAMPLES::
code::
PlotLib.ticks(2.4, 4.0, 6);          // [2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0]
PlotLib.ticks(0, 22050, 6);          // 0, 5000, 10000, 15000, 20000
PlotLib.map(5, 0, 10, 100, 200);     // 150
PlotLib.map(99, 0, 10, 100, 200);    // 200, clipped
PlotLib.extent([3, -1, 7]);          // [-1, 7]
PlotLib.histogram([0.05, 0.15, 0.15], 0, 1, 10);
PlotLib.ampDb(0.5).round(0.01);      // -6.02
::
